(_gn2.chapters = _gn2.chapters || []).push(
  { n:26, summary:"A famine sends Isaac to Gerar, where the LORD appears to him, tells him not to go down to Egypt, and renews the oath sworn to Abraham, promising that his seed will multiply and that all nations will be blessed through it. Like his father, Isaac passes Rebekah off as his sister, and King Abimelech, discovering the truth, charges his people not to touch either of them. Isaac sows and reaps a hundredfold, provoking Philistine envy, and Abimelech asks him to leave. Isaac reopens the wells Abraham dug, and when herdmen strive over two of them he names them Esek and Sitnah, moving on until at Rehoboth the LORD makes room. From Beer-sheba, where the LORD again appears and blesses him, Abimelech comes to seek a peace oath, which Isaac grants with a feast. The chapter closes with Esau marrying two Hittite wives, a grief of mind to Isaac and Rebekah.",
  context:"Isaac sojourns in Gerar among the Philistines during a famine, later dwelling in the valley of Gerar and at Beer-sheba.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 26:3", text:"Sojourn in this land, and I will be with thee, and will bless thee; for unto thee, and unto thy seed, I will give all these countries, and I will perform the oath which I sware unto Abraham thy father;" },
    { ref:"Genesis 26:12", text:"Then Isaac sowed in that land, and received in the same year an hundredfold: and the LORD blessed him." },
    { ref:"Genesis 26:24", text:"And the LORD appeared unto him the same night, and said, I am the God of Abraham thy father: fear not, for I am with thee, and will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham's sake." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God keeps covenant promises with the next generation, and peacemaking can turn rivals into allies.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did the LORD tell Isaac to sojourn instead of going down to Egypt?", choices:["In Gerar, among the Philistines","In the city of Haran","In the land of Goshen","In a cave at Machpelah"], a:0, why:"The LORD said, Go not down into Egypt... Sojourn in this land; and Isaac dwelt in Gerar, where Abimelech was king." },
    { q:"Why did Isaac keep digging new wells and moving on?", choices:["Abimelech ordered every well sealed with earth","Herdmen of Gerar strove over two wells before he found one at Rehoboth that they did not contest","He wanted to find his way back to Egypt","The water in each well ran dry"], a:1, why:"The herdmen of Gerar strove over the wells Esek and Sitnah; at Rehoboth he said, For now the LORD hath made room for us." },
    { q:"What principle does Isaac's willingness to move rather than strive over water support?", choices:["Blessings come only to those who fight for them","Relocating is always better than negotiating","It is weakness to ever yield to others","The LORD can make room for those who avoid contention and keep building"], a:2, why:"Isaac dug again and again, and after the striving ceased he named the well Rehoboth, saying the LORD had made room for them; that same night the LORD appeared and blessed him." }
  ] },
  { n:27, summary:"Old and nearly blind, Isaac prepares to bless Esau after a meal of venison. Rebekah overhears and quickly arranges a deception: Jacob brings goat meat dressed as venison, wearing Esau's clothing with goatskins on his hands and neck. Though Isaac notes Jacob's voice, the disguise deceives him, and he grants Jacob the blessing of plenty, lordship over nations, and rule over his brethren. Esau returns moments later; Isaac trembles but will not revoke the blessing, and for Esau he foretells a harder lot, living by the sword, serving his brother, yet one day breaking the yoke. Esau resolves to kill Jacob after his father's death, so Rebekah sends Jacob to her brother Laban in Haran, telling Isaac she cannot bear to see Jacob marry a daughter of Heth.",
  context:"The aging Isaac, living in Canaan, gives a deathbed blessing that sends Jacob fleeing to Laban in Haran while Esau plans revenge.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 27:29", text:"Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee: be lord over thy brethren, and let thy mother's sons bow down to thee: cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee." },
    { ref:"Genesis 27:33", text:"And Isaac trembled very exceedingly, and said, Who? where is he that hath taken venison, and brought it me, and I have eaten of all before thou camest, and have blessed him? yea, and he shall be blessed." },
    { ref:"Genesis 27:41", text:"And Esau hated Jacob because of the blessing wherewith his father blessed him: and Esau said in his heart, The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then will I slay my brother Jacob." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Deception and favoritism tear families apart; blessings cannot be seized without cost.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How did Rebekah disguise Jacob to pass as Esau?", choices:["She dyed his skin and shaved his head","She put Esau's goodly raiment on him and covered his hands and neck with skins of the kids of the goats","She had a servant speak in his place","She gave him a false beard"], a:1, why:"Rebekah took goodly raiment of Esau and put the skins of the kids of the goats upon Jacob's hands and the smooth of his neck." },
    { q:"What did Isaac do when the deception came to light before Esau?", choices:["He cursed Jacob and revoked the blessing","He blamed Rebekah openly before both sons","He trembled very exceedingly but said of Jacob, yea, and he shall be blessed","He fell silent and refused to speak again"], a:2, why:"Isaac trembled exceedingly, yet stood by what he had spoken: he had eaten and blessed Jacob, and he shall be blessed." },
    { q:"What does this chapter warn about obtaining blessings through deceit?", choices:["Clever planning earns lasting honor","Deceit may capture the words of a blessing while filling the family with division, grief, and fear","God overlooks trickery in sacred matters","The younger should always yield the blessing"], a:3, why:"The trick gained Jacob the blessing but cost him his home: Esau purposed to slay him, and Rebekah sent him away to Haran." }
  ] },
  { n:28, summary:"Isaac sends Jacob to Padan-aram with a charge not to marry a Canaanite woman, blessing him with the blessing of Abraham and the promise of the land. Esau, learning what pleased his father, takes Mahalath, a daughter of Ishmael, as a wife. Journeying toward Haran, Jacob sleeps at a certain place with a stone for a pillow and dreams of a ladder reaching to heaven, with angels ascending and descending on it and the LORD standing above. The LORD promises him the land, innumerable seed, that all families of the earth will be blessed in him, and His own companionship until all is performed. Waking awestruck, Jacob names the place Beth-el, sets up his stone as a pillar, pours oil on it, and vows that the LORD will be his God and that he will give a tenth of all he receives.",
  context:"Jacob travels from Beer-sheba toward Haran, stopping overnight at a place he names Beth-el, formerly called Luz.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 28:12", text:"And he dreamed, and behold a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it." },
    { ref:"Genesis 28:15", text:"And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of." },
    { ref:"Genesis 28:22", text:"And this stone, which I have set for a pillar, shall be God's house: and of all that thou shalt give me I will surely give the tenth unto thee." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord meets those who walk toward Him in covenant, turning ordinary places into sacred ground.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did Jacob see in his dream on the way to Haran?", choices:["Sheaves bowing in a field","A ship sailing for Egypt","A ladder set on earth reaching to heaven, with angels of God ascending and descending","A river dividing four ways"], a:2, why:"He dreamed of a ladder whose top reached to heaven, and the angels of God ascended and descended on it, the LORD standing above." },
    { q:"How did Jacob respond the morning after the dream?", choices:["He hurried on without stopping","He offered a flock in sacrifice","He hid the stone where he had slept","He set his pillow-stone up as a pillar, poured oil on it, and called the place Beth-el"], a:3, why:"He rose up early, took the stone he had put for his pillows, set it for a pillar, poured oil on it, and named the place Beth-el, the house of God." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's vow — that the LORD would be his God, and that he would surely give the tenth — model?", choices:["Covenant worship includes yielding ourselves and our substance to the Lord","God requires payment before granting blessings","Tithing applies only to grain harvests","Sacred experiences should be kept private"], a:0, why:"Jacob vowed that the LORD would be his God, the stone would be God's house, and a tenth of all given to him would be returned to God." }
  ] },
  { n:29, summary:"Arriving at a well in Haran, Jacob rolls away its stone to water Rachel's flock and is welcomed into Laban's home. Asked what wages he wants, Jacob offers seven years' service for Rachel, and the years seem but a few days for his love. At the wedding feast Laban substitutes his elder daughter Leah, pleading that the younger is not given before the firstborn, and Jacob must serve seven more years for Rachel, whom he loves more than Leah. Laban gives each daughter a handmaid, Zilpah for Leah and Bilhah for Rachel. Because Leah was hated, the LORD opens her womb; she bears Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah, each name recording her hope that her husband will now love her, while Rachel remains barren.",
  context:"Jacob serves Laban in Haran, in Padan-aram, where he marries Leah and Rachel, Laban's two daughters.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 29:20", text:"And Jacob served seven years for Rachel; and they seemed unto him but a few days, for the love he had to her." },
    { ref:"Genesis 29:25", text:"And it came to pass, that in the morning, behold, it was Leah: and he said to Laban, What is this thou hast done unto me? did not I serve with thee for Rachel? wherefore then hast thou beguiled me?" },
    { ref:"Genesis 29:31", text:"And when the LORD saw that Leah was hated, he opened her womb: but Rachel was barren." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord sees the overlooked and blesses them even when people play favorites.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What arrangement did Jacob make with Laban for Rachel?", choices:["To serve ten years tending flocks","To pay a hundred pieces of silver","To clear Laban's fields for planting","To serve seven years"], a:3, why:"Jacob said, I will serve thee seven years for Rachel thy younger daughter, and served; the years seemed but a few days for his love." },
    { q:"How did Laban justify giving him Leah instead?", choices:["It must not be so done in our country, to give the younger before the firstborn","Leah had first claim as Jacob's chosen bride","Rachel had refused the marriage","A dream told him to do it"], a:0, why:"Laban answered that the younger could not be given before the firstborn, then promised Rachel also for seven more years of service." },
    { q:"What does the LORD's regard for Leah — he saw she was hated, and opened her womb — teach?", choices:["God's blessings follow popularity","God notices the afflicted and shows them tender mercy","Unloved people have no place in covenant families","Children are prizes in a rivalry"], a:1, why:"When the LORD saw that Leah was hated, he opened her womb, and her sons' names record her hope and God's regard for her." }
  ] },
  { n:30, summary:"Rachel, childless, envies Leah and gives Jacob her handmaid Bilhah, who bears Dan and Naphtali; Leah, who has stopped bearing, answers with Zilpah, mother of Gad and Asher. After Reuben finds mandrakes, Leah trades them for a night with Jacob and bears Issachar and Zebulun, then Dinah. At last God remembers Rachel, and she bears Joseph. When Joseph is born, Jacob asks to leave, but Laban, blessed for Jacob's sake, persuades him to stay and lets him set his own wages: the speckled, spotted, and brown cattle. Jacob uses peeled rods and selective breeding so the stronger animals become his, and he increases exceedingly with flocks, servants, camels, and asses.",
  context:"The chapter stays in Laban's household at Haran, where Jacob's children are born and his flocks multiply before the return to Canaan.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 30:22", text:"And God remembered Rachel, and God hearkened to her, and opened her womb." },
    { ref:"Genesis 30:27", text:"And Laban said unto him, I pray thee, if I have found favour in thine eyes, tarry: for I have learned by experience that the LORD hath blessed me for thy sake." },
    { ref:"Genesis 30:43", text:"And the man increased exceedingly, and had much cattle, and maidservants, and menservants, and camels, and asses." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God can turn unfair dealing into blessing and remembers those who wait on Him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whose handmaid did Rachel give Jacob so she might have children by her?", choices:["Bilhah","Zilpah","Dinah","Serah"], a:0, why:"Rachel gave Bilhah her handmaid to Jacob, and Bilhah bore Dan and Naphtali." },
    { q:"What wages did Jacob propose when Laban pressed him to stay?", choices:["A tithe of Laban's grain","A house in Haran for his family","The speckled and spotted cattle and the brown among the sheep as his hire","Freedom for his wives' handmaids"], a:2, why:"Jacob asked that the speckled, spotted, and brown animals be removed and that such born afterward be his hire; Laban agreed." },
    { q:"What does the arc from Rachel's cry — Give me children, or else I die — to God remembering her teach?", choices:["Envy is a safe way to measure progress","Comparison settles every family question","Demands hasten God's blessings","Envy breeds misery, while God hears prayer in His own time"], a:1, why:"Rachel envied Leah and cried out in anguish, but only after years did God remember Rachel and open her womb, and she bare Joseph." }
  ] },
  { n:31, summary:"Laban's sons accuse Jacob of taking away all that was their father's, and Laban's countenance is no longer friendly; the LORD commands Jacob to return to the land of his fathers. Jacob calls Rachel and Leah to the field and rehearses twenty years of service, fourteen for the daughters and six for the cattle, with wages changed ten times, while God kept Laban from hurting him and showed him in a dream that the ringstraked and speckled cattle were his. The sisters agree their father has sold them and devoured their money. Jacob sets out for Canaan while Laban is away shearing sheep; Rachel steals her father's images. Laban pursues seven days, but God warns him in a dream to speak neither good nor bad; the images are not found, hidden under Rachel as she sits on them. Jacob recounts his toil, drought and frost endured, and they raise a heap of stones as a witness covenant, Galeed, vowing the LORD watch between them, then eat bread and part.",
  context:"Jacob flees Padan-aram for Canaan; Laban overtakes him in mount Gilead, where the two men make a covenant and part.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 31:3", text:"And the LORD said unto Jacob, Return unto the land of thy fathers, and to thy kindred; and I will be with thee." },
    { ref:"Genesis 31:24", text:"And God came to Laban the Syrian in a dream by night, and said unto him, Take heed that thou speak not to Jacob either good or bad." },
    { ref:"Genesis 31:42", text:"Except the God of my father, the God of Abraham, and the fear of Isaac, had been with me, surely thou hadst sent me away now empty. God hath seen mine affliction and the labour of my hands, and rebuked thee yesternight." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God watches over those who keep covenant with Him, even when others deal unfairly.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How did Jacob sum up his twenty years with Laban?", choices:["Ten years for cattle and ten for land","Fourteen years for the daughters and six years for the cattle, with wages changed ten times","Seven years for Rachel and none for Leah","Twenty years as a free partner sharing profits"], a:1, why:"Jacob said, I served thee fourteen years for thy two daughters, and six years for thy cattle: and thou hast changed my wages ten times." },
    { q:"Why did Jacob slip away without telling Laban?", choices:["Laban had forbidden any departure","He wanted to surprise Esau on the road","He was ashamed of his wealth","He feared Laban would take his daughters away by force"], a:3, why:"Jacob answered Laban, Because I was afraid: for I said, Peradventure thou wouldest take by force thy daughters from me." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's testimony — Except the God of my father had been with me, surely thou hadst sent me away now empty — teach?", choices:["Wealth proves God's favor","Success comes from outworking opponents","God's presence sustains His servants through years of unfair treatment","Family disputes end with no winners"], a:2, why:"Jacob credited the God of Abraham and the fear of Isaac for keeping Laban from sending him away empty, seeing his affliction and the labour of his hands." }
  ] },
  { n:32, summary:"Angels meet Jacob on the way, and he names the place Mahanaim. Fearing his brother, Jacob sends messengers to Esau in Edom, and they return reporting Esau comes with four hundred men. Jacob divides his company into two bands, prays with striking humility — I am not worthy of the least of all the mercies — and pleads for deliverance, recalling the LORD's own promises. He then sends a lavish present of goats, sheep, camels, cattle, and asses in droves ahead to appease Esau. That night, having crossed his family over the brook Jabbok, Jacob is left alone and wrestles with a man until daybreak; when the man touches and shrinks Jacob's thigh, Jacob still will not let go without a blessing. He is told his name is now Israel, for as a prince he has power with God and with men, and he names the place Peniel.",
  context:"Journeying home from Haran, Jacob camps by the brook Jabbok as he prepares to meet Esau, who dwells in the land of Seir, Edom.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 32:24", text:"And Jacob was left alone; and there wrestled a man with him until the breaking of the day." },
    { ref:"Genesis 32:28", text:"And he said, Thy name shall be called no more Jacob, but Israel: for as a prince hast thou power with God and with men, and hast prevailed." },
    { ref:"Genesis 32:30", text:"And Jacob called the name of the place Peniel: for I have seen God face to face, and my life is preserved." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Humble, persistent prayer can prepare us to face the people and problems we fear most.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many men came with Esau to meet Jacob?", choices:["One hundred","Two hundred","Four hundred","Twelve"], a:2, why:"The messengers returned saying, He cometh to meet thee, and four hundred men with him, and Jacob was greatly afraid." },
    { q:"How did Jacob first prepare for the meeting with Esau?", choices:["He divided his people and flocks into two bands, so that if one were smitten the other could escape","He built fortifications around the camp","He sent the women back to Haran","He challenged Esau to single combat"], a:0, why:"Jacob divided the people, flocks, herds, and camels into two companies, praying, Deliver me, I pray thee, from the hand of my brother." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's night of wrestling — I will not let thee go, except thou bless me — illustrate about seeking blessings?", choices:["Physical struggle earns spiritual rewards","Blessings come without effort to covenant people","God refuses those who ask directly","Prevailing with God comes through humble persistence that does not quit"], a:3, why:"Alone at Peniel, Jacob held on until dawn despite his injured thigh, and was blessed and renamed Israel, for as a prince he had power with God and with men." }
  ] },
  { n:33, summary:"Esau approaches with his four hundred men, but the feared revenge never comes: he runs to meet Jacob, embraces him, falls on his neck, kisses him, and they weep. Jacob introduces his family, the handmaids and their children, then Leah with hers, with Rachel and Joseph last, and presents his gift, urging Esau to keep it because God has dealt graciously with him; Esau accepts. Declining Esau's offer to travel together because the children and flocks are tender, Jacob promises to follow at a gentle pace. Esau returns to Seir, while Jacob builds a house and booths at Succoth, then buys land near Shechem from the children of Hamor for a hundred pieces of money and erects an altar he calls El-elohe-Israel.",
  context:"The reunion takes place as Jacob enters Canaan from Padan-aram; he then camps at Succoth and buys land near Shechem.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 33:4", text:"And Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck, and kissed him: and they wept." },
    { ref:"Genesis 33:10", text:"And Jacob said, Nay, I pray thee, if now I have found grace in thy sight, then receive my present at my hand: for therefore I have seen thy face, as though I had seen the face of God, and thou wast pleased with me." },
    { ref:"Genesis 33:20", text:"And he erected there an altar, and called it El-elohe-Israel." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Humble goodwill and generosity can dissolve hatred that years of fear have built.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What happened when Esau reached Jacob?", choices:["He demanded the gift as payment","He arrested Jacob's herdmen","He turned his four hundred men on the camp","He ran to meet him, embraced him, fell on his neck, and kissed him"], a:3, why:"Esau ran to meet Jacob, embraced him, fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept." },
    { q:"On what basis did Jacob press the present on Esau?", choices:["Esau's men were hungry","Because God had dealt graciously with him, and he had enough","Egyptian custom required gifts","A vow made at Beth-el"], a:1, why:"Jacob urged him, Take, I pray thee, my blessing... because God hath dealt graciously with me, and because I have enough; and Esau took it." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's bowing seven times and urging reconciliation model for making peace?", choices:["True reconciliation requires humility, not just words","Peace is best bought with wealth","Justice always requires repayment first","Forgiveness means forgetting everything at once"], a:0, why:"Jacob bowed himself to the ground seven times, called the gift a blessing, and spoke of seeing Esau's face as seeing the face of God." }
  ] },
  { n:34, summary:"Dinah, Leah's daughter, is defiled by Shechem the son of Hamor, a Hivite prince, who then loves her and asks for her as a wife. Hamor proposes intermarriage and shared land; Shechem offers any dowry. Jacob's sons answer deceitfully, requiring every male of the city to be circumcised; on the third day, while the men are sore, Simeon and Levi kill all the males, take Dinah, and the other sons spoil the city, taking wealth, wives, and children. Jacob fears the Canaanites and Perizzites will gather against his small household, but the brothers ask whether their sister should be treated as a harlot.",
  context:"The episode occurs near Shechem in Canaan, where Jacob had bought a parcel of land from the children of Hamor.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 34:7", text:"And the sons of Jacob came out of the field when they heard it: and the men were grieved, and they were very wroth, because he had wrought folly in Israel in lying with Jacob's daughter; which thing ought not to be done." },
    { ref:"Genesis 34:13", text:"And the sons of Jacob answered Shechem and Hamor his father deceitfully, and said, because he had defiled Dinah their sister:" },
    { ref:"Genesis 34:30", text:"And Jacob said to Simeon and Levi, Ye have troubled me to make me to stink among the inhabitants of the land, among the Canaanites and the Perizzites: and I being few in number, they shall gather themselves together against me, and slay me; and I shall be destroyed, I and my house." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Anger and deceit multiply wrongs; vengeance carried out treacherously brings new trouble on everyone.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which two sons of Jacob attacked the men of Shechem?", choices:["Simeon and Levi","Reuben and Judah","Issachar and Zebulun","Dan and Naphtali"], a:0, why:"On the third day, when the men were sore, Simeon and Levi, Dinah's brethren, took each man his sword and slew all the males." },
    { q:"What condition did Jacob's sons set for the proposed marriages?", choices:["That Hamor resign as prince","That the city pay tribute in cattle each year","That Shechem serve Jacob for seven years","That every male of their city be circumcised"], a:3, why:"They answered deceitfully that they could not give Dinah to one uncircumcised; if every male were circumcised, then would they become one people." },
    { q:"What warning does Simeon and Levi's revenge carry about anger?", choices:["Anger reliably produces justice","Fierce wrath over a real wrong can become cruelty that endangers everyone around it","Family honor excuses any response","Justice delayed is justice denied"], a:1, why:"Their deceit and slaughter made Jacob say, Ye have troubled me, fearing the Canaanites would gather and destroy his small house." }
  ] },
  { n:35, summary:"God commands Jacob to go up to Beth-el and build an altar, and Jacob orders his household to put away their strange gods, which he buries under an oak by Shechem. The terror of God keeps surrounding cities from pursuing them. At Beth-el, Deborah, Rebekah's nurse, dies; God appears to Jacob again, blesses him, and confirms his new name, Israel, promising that nations and kings will come from him and repeating the land promise. Journeying on toward Ephrath, Rachel dies in hard labor, naming her son Ben-oni; Jacob calls him Benjamin and sets a pillar on Rachel's grave. Reuben's sin with Bilhah is noted, the twelve sons of Jacob are listed, and the chapter closes at Hebron with Isaac's death at a hundred and fourscore years, buried by Esau and Jacob.",
  context:"The family moves from Shechem to Beth-el and on toward Ephrath, ending at Mamre in Hebron, where Isaac dies.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 35:1", text:"And God said unto Jacob, Arise, go up to Beth-el, and dwell there: and make there an altar unto God, that appeared unto thee when thou fleddest from the face of Esau thy brother." },
    { ref:"Genesis 35:10", text:"And God said unto him, Thy name is Jacob: thy name shall not be called any more Jacob, but Israel shall be thy name: and he called his name Israel." },
    { ref:"Genesis 35:18", text:"And it came to pass, as her soul was in departing, (for she died) that she called his name Ben-oni: but his father called him Benjamin." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Returning to covenant with God means putting away everything that competes with Him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What name did God confirm as Jacob's at Beth-el?", choices:["Abraham","Israel","Peniel","Galeed"], a:1, why:"God said, Thy name shall not be called any more Jacob, but Israel shall be thy name: and he called his name Israel." },
    { q:"What did Jacob command his household before going up to Beth-el?", choices:["To put away the strange gods among them, be clean, and change their garments","To divide the flocks among the sons","To send messengers ahead to Esau","To fast for seven days"], a:0, why:"Jacob said, Put away the strange gods that are among you; they gave him the gods and earrings, which he hid under the oak by Shechem." },
    { q:"What do the deaths recorded around Beth-el — Deborah, Rachel, and Isaac — show about the Lord's covenant?", choices:["Covenant blessings prevent grief","Sorrow means God has withdrawn","God keeps His promises through every season of loss, across generations","Family ties end at the grave"], a:2, why:"Amid deaths and births, God confirmed Israel's name and the land promise; Jacob moved on, buried Rachel, and came to his father Isaac before he died." }
  ] },
  { n:36, summary:"The generations of Esau, called Edom, are recorded here. His three wives — Adah the Hittite, Aholibamah of the Hivites, and Bashemath of Ishmael's line — his sons born in Canaan, and his move to mount Seir, because his and Jacob's substance was too great for the land to bear them together, open the account. It then lists Esau's grandsons, the dukes of the sons of Esau, the dukes of the Horites who first inhabited Seir, and the kings who reigned in Edom before any king ruled over Israel, closing with dukes named according to their families and places. Esau is identified as the father of the Edomites.",
  context:"The genealogy follows Esau's line into mount Seir, the land of Edom, where his descendants become dukes and kings.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 36:1", text:"Now these are the generations of Esau, who is Edom." },
    { ref:"Genesis 36:8", text:"Thus dwelt Esau in mount Seir: Esau is Edom." },
    { ref:"Genesis 36:31", text:"And these are the kings that reigned in the land of Edom, before there reigned any king over the children of Israel." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God's promises to Abraham of nations and kings held true even in Esau's line, a people with lands and rulers.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where did Esau settle with his family and substance?", choices:["The land of Goshen","The plains of Mamre","Mount Seir","The valley of Gerar"], a:2, why:"Esau took all his house and goods and went into the country from the face of his brother Jacob; thus dwelt Esau in mount Seir." },
    { q:"Why did Esau and Jacob not dwell together in Canaan?", choices:["Their herdsmen quarreled over the wells","Their riches and cattle were more than the land could bear while they dwelt together","Jacob's sons refused to live near Edomites","A famine struck Canaan and drove Esau out"], a:1, why:"Their riches were more than that they might dwell together, and the land where they were strangers could not bear them because of their cattle." },
    { q:"What does the chapter record about kings in Edom?", choices:["Edom had no kings at all","Israel's king ruled Edom directly","Edom's first king was Jacob's grandson","Kings reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Israel"], a:3, why:"The chapter lists the kings that reigned in the land of Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Israel, then more dukes of Esau." }
  ] },
  { n:37, summary:"Seventeen-year-old Joseph feeds flocks with his brothers and brings their evil report to his father; Israel's special love, shown in a coat of many colours, breeds hatred. Joseph's two dreams, of sheaves bowing to his sheaf and of the sun, moon, and eleven stars making obeisance, deepen the envy, though his father observes the saying. Sent from Hebron to check on the flocks, Joseph finds his brothers at Dothan, where they conspire to kill him. Reuben persuades them to cast him into a dry pit instead, planning a rescue, but the brothers strip the coat, sell him to Ishmeelite merchants for twenty pieces of silver, dip the coat in goat's blood, and let Jacob conclude a beast devoured him. Jacob mourns, refusing comfort, while Joseph is sold in Egypt to Potiphar, captain of the guard.",
  context:"The account moves from Hebron through Shechem and Dothan in Canaan and ends with Joseph sold in Egypt to Potiphar, captain of the guard.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 37:3", text:"Now Israel loved Joseph more than all his children, because he was the son of his old age: and he made him a coat of many colours." },
    { ref:"Genesis 37:7", text:"For, behold, we were binding sheaves in the field, and, lo, my sheaf arose, and also stood upright; and, behold, your sheaves stood round about, and made obeisance to my sheaf." },
    { ref:"Genesis 37:28", text:"Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Unchecked jealousy can harden into cruelty, yet God's purposes still move through the wrongs of others.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"For how much was Joseph sold to the Ishmeelites?", choices:["Ten pieces of silver","Thirty pieces of silver","A coat and a pair of shoes","Twenty pieces of silver"], a:3, why:"Midianite merchantmen lifted Joseph out of the pit and sold him to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver, and he was brought into Egypt." },
    { q:"Why did Reuben tell the brothers to cast Joseph into the pit rather than kill him?", choices:["He thought the pit a fitting punishment","He wanted to rid him out of their hands and deliver him to his father again","He hoped traders would arrive quickly","He feared Jacob would reward the brothers"], a:2, why:"Reuben heard the plot, said, Let us not kill him, and planned secretly to rid Joseph from their hands and restore him to his father." },
    { q:"What do the brothers' words — come... and we shall see what will become of his dreams — warn against?", choices:["Mocking the gifts and callings God gives to others","Telling a father about a sibling's conduct","Working for the family business","Sharing dreams at family gatherings"], a:0, why:"They conspired against the dreamer, but the dreams they scorned later came true, as Genesis 42 shows them bowing before Joseph." }
  ] },
  { n:38, summary:"Judah leaves his brothers and marries a daughter of the Canaanite Shuah, who bears him three sons: Er, Onan, and Shelah. Er and Onan both die for wickedness before the LORD, and Judah, fearing for Shelah, sends Tamar, the widow, back to her father's house. After Judah's wife dies, he goes up to Timnath to his sheepshearers with his friend Hirah the Adullamite. When Shelah is grown and still not given to her, Tamar disguises herself by the way to Timnath and, by receiving Judah's signet, bracelets, and staff as a pledge, conceives by him. Confronted about three months later, Judah acknowledges the pledge, saying she has been more righteous than he because he withheld Shelah. She bears twin sons, Pharez and Zarah.",
  context:"The account follows Judah among the Canaanites at Adullam and Timnath while Joseph is carried down to Egypt.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 38:8", text:"And Judah said unto Onan, Go in unto thy brother's wife, and marry her, and raise up seed to thy brother." },
    { ref:"Genesis 38:26", text:"And Judah acknowledged them, and said, She hath been more righteous than I; because that I gave her not to Shelah my son. And he knew her again no more." },
    { ref:"Genesis 38:30", text:"And afterward came out his brother, that had the scarlet thread upon his hand: and his name was called Zarah." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Honest acknowledgment of wrong, however late, is the beginning of change: she hath been more righteous than I.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What three things did Tamar ask Judah for as a pledge?", choices:["His signet, his bracelets, and his staff","His ring, his robe, and his sandals","His sword, his tent, and his servants","His kid, his coins, and his cloak"], a:0, why:"Tamar asked, Thy signet, and thy bracelets, and thy staff that is in thine hand; and he gave them to her." },
    { q:"Why did Judah say Tamar had been more righteous than he?", choices:["She had cared for his dying wife","Because he had not given her Shelah his son as promised","She had refused any payment","She confessed before being accused"], a:1, why:"Judah acknowledged his signet, bracelets, and staff, and said, She hath been more righteous than I; because that I gave her not to Shelah my son." },
    { q:"What does the chapter — Er's and Onan's deaths, Judah's confession, and the birth of Pharez and Zarah — establish?", choices:["Wickedness usually escapes notice","Righteousness is inherited from fathers","Comfort matters more than covenant duty","The Lord judges wickedness, and truth must be acknowledged, however late"], a:3, why:"The LORD slew Er and Onan for wickedness, and Judah's own admission that Tamar was more righteous than he opened the way for the twins' birth." }
  ] },
  { n:39, summary:"Sold to Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh and captain of the guard, Joseph prospers because the LORD is with him; his master sees it, makes him overseer of his house, and leaves everything in his hand, so that the LORD blesses the Egyptian's house for Joseph's sake. When Potiphar's wife demands that Joseph lie with her, he refuses — how can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God? — and turns down her repeated advances. Seized by his garment one day when no men are in the house, he flees, leaving it behind; she uses the garment to accuse him falsely, and Potiphar's wrath lands Joseph in the prison where the king's prisoners are bound. Even there the LORD is with him, giving him favour with the keeper, who commits the prisoners to his hand, and the LORD makes all he does to prosper.",
  context:"The account is set in Egypt, in the house of Potiphar, captain of Pharaoh's guard, and then in the prison where the king's prisoners were bound.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 39:2", text:"And the LORD was with Joseph, and he was a prosperous man; and he was in the house of his master the Egyptian." },
    { ref:"Genesis 39:9", text:"There is none greater in this house than I; neither hath he kept back any thing from me but thee, because thou art his wife: how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?" },
    { ref:"Genesis 39:21", text:"But the LORD was with Joseph, and shewed him mercy, and gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Integrity may cost comfort and position, but the Lord remains with those who refuse to sin against Him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What trust did Potiphar place in Joseph?", choices:["He made him captain of Pharaoh's guard","He made him overseer of his house and left all that he had in Joseph's hand","He assigned him only the outer fields","He kept him from meeting his wife"], a:1, why:"His master saw that the LORD made all Joseph did to prosper, so he made him overseer over his house and put all he had into his hand." },
    { q:"Why did Joseph refuse Potiphar's wife?", choices:["He feared losing wages","He expected a promotion elsewhere","She had treated his family badly","His master had kept back nothing from him but her, and the act would be sin against God"], a:3, why:"Joseph answered, Behold, my master wotteth not what is with me in the house... how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?" },
    { q:"What does the refrain the LORD was with Joseph — in the house and again in the prison — teach?", choices:["God's presence does not depend on favorable circumstances","Prosperity proves God's approval","Prison is always punishment for sin","Fame follows faithfulness"], a:0, why:"The LORD was with Joseph as a prosperous servant and again shewed him mercy in prison, giving him favour with the keeper of the prison." }
  ] },
  { n:40, summary:"Pharaoh's chief butler and chief baker offend him and are confined in the prison where Joseph is bound, and the captain of the guard charges Joseph to serve them. Each dreams in one night and wakes sad, for there is no interpreter; Joseph answers that interpretations belong to God and asks them to tell him their dreams. The butler's vine with three branches means within three days Pharaoh will restore him; the baker's three baskets, eaten by birds, mean he will be hanged. Joseph asks the butler to mention him to Pharaoh and bring him out, having been stolen away and done nothing worthy of the dungeon. On Pharaoh's birthday the predictions come true exactly, but the butler forgets Joseph.",
  context:"The account takes place in the Egyptian prison where the king's prisoners were bound, under the captain of the guard.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 40:8", text:"And they said unto him, We have dreamed a dream, and there is no interpreter of it. And Joseph said unto them, Do not interpretations belong to God? tell me them, I pray you." },
    { ref:"Genesis 40:14", text:"But think on me when it shall be well with thee, and shew kindness, I pray thee, unto me, and make mention of me unto Pharaoh, and bring me out of this house:" },
    { ref:"Genesis 40:23", text:"Yet did not the chief butler remember Joseph, but forgat him." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God can reveal what is ahead; serving faithfully while we wait is itself an offering.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did the chief butler see in his dream?", choices:["Birds eating bread from a basket","A withered tree by a river","A vine with three branches that budded and brought forth ripe grapes, with Pharaoh's cup in his hand","Seven fat and seven lean cattle"], a:2, why:"The butler saw a vine with three branches bringing forth ripe grapes; he pressed them into Pharaoh's cup and gave it to Pharaoh." },
    { q:"What did Joseph say when told there was no interpreter?", choices:["Do not interpretations belong to God? tell me them, I pray you","Only Pharaoh's magicians may interpret dreams","Dreams without a fee mean nothing","He could guess by their faces"], a:0, why:"Joseph said, Do not interpretations belong to God? and then interpreted each dream, crediting God rather than himself." },
    { q:"What does the butler's forgetting Joseph — yet did not the chief butler remember Joseph, but forgat him — teach about hope?", choices:["The powerful always repay kindness","Human help can fail; the Lord's timing governs deliverance","Patience plays no part in affliction","Prisoners should never ask for aid"], a:1, why:"Though Joseph asked to be mentioned to Pharaoh, the butler forgot him for two full years, until God's time came in Pharaoh's dreams." }
  ] },
  { n:41, summary:"Two full years later Pharaoh dreams: seven fat kine eaten by seven lean ones from the river, and seven good ears devoured by seven thin, blasted ears. None of Egypt's magicians can interpret, and at last the chief butler remembers his faults and Joseph. Brought hastily from the dungeon, Joseph tells Pharaoh, It is not in me: God shall give Pharaoh an answer of peace. The doubled dream means the thing is established by God: seven years of plenty, then seven years of famine so grievous the plenty will be forgotten. Joseph advises appointing a discreet and wise man to store a fifth in the plenteous years. Pharaoh, seeing the Spirit of God in him, sets Joseph over all Egypt, giving him his ring, fine linen, a gold chain, the second chariot, the name Zaphnath-paaneah, and Asenath, daughter of the priest of On. Thirty years old, Joseph stores grain without number; sons Manasseh and Ephraim are born before famine strikes all lands, and bread is found only in Egypt.",
  context:"The scene is Pharaoh's court in Egypt; Joseph is lifted from the king's prison to rule the land through plenty and famine.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 41:16", text:"And Joseph answered Pharaoh, saying, It is not in me: God shall give Pharaoh an answer of peace." },
    { ref:"Genesis 41:32", text:"And for that the dream was doubled unto Pharaoh twice; it is because the thing is established by God, and God will shortly bring it to pass." },
    { ref:"Genesis 41:41", text:"And Pharaoh said unto Joseph, See, I have set thee over all the land of Egypt." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God gives wisdom to prepare for the future, and to Him belongs the credit.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How old was Joseph when he stood before Pharaoh king of Egypt?", choices:["Seventeen","Twenty","Twenty-five","Thirty"], a:3, why:"Joseph was thirty years old when he stood before Pharaoh, thirteen years after his brothers sold him at seventeen." },
    { q:"Why did Pharaoh's dream come twice — as kine and then as ears of corn?", choices:["Pharaoh forgot the first version","The two dreams conflicted","The dream was doubled because the thing is established by God, and God will shortly bring it to pass","One dream belonged to the butler"], a:2, why:"Joseph explained both dreams were one: seven years of plenty and seven of famine, the doubling establishing that God would shortly do it." },
    { q:"What does Joseph's answer — It is not in me: God shall give Pharaoh an answer of peace — model?", choices:["Sharing credit with magicians","Attributing one's gifts humbly to God","Refusing to serve rulers","Interpreting dreams for payment"], a:1, why:"When Pharaoh said he had heard Joseph could interpret, Joseph answered that it was not in him: God would give Pharaoh an answer of peace." }
  ] },
  { n:42, summary:"Famine drives Jacob to send ten sons to Egypt for corn, keeping Benjamin home. As governor of the land, Joseph sells to all comers, and his brothers bow before him with their faces to the earth, fulfilling the early dreams though they do not know him. He speaks roughly, calls them spies, and insists they prove themselves by bringing their youngest brother. Three days in ward later, he lets nine return with corn while Simeon is bound before their eyes. Overheard speaking in their own language, the brothers confess their guilt over Joseph's anguish, and Joseph weeps. He fills their sacks and secretly returns every man's money; finding it on the way and at home, they and Jacob are afraid. Jacob refuses Reuben's offer of his own sons as surety: Benjamin will not go, for Joseph is not, and Simeon is not.",
  context:"During the famine in Canaan, Jacob's ten eldest sons travel to Egypt for corn, where Joseph governs the land for Pharaoh.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 42:6", text:"And Joseph was the governor over the land, and he it was that sold to all the people of the land: and Joseph's brethren came, and bowed down themselves before him with their faces to the earth." },
    { ref:"Genesis 42:21", text:"And they said one to another, We are verily guilty concerning our brother, in that we saw the anguish of his soul, when he besought us, and we would not hear; therefore is this distress come upon us." },
    { ref:"Genesis 42:24", text:"And he turned himself about from them, and wept; and returned to them again, and communed with them, and took from them Simeon, and bound him before their eyes." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Guilt returns to conscience; honesty before God begins with admitting past wrongs.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which brother did Joseph keep in Egypt while the rest carried corn home?", choices:["Simeon","Reuben","Levi","Judah"], a:0, why:"Joseph took Simeon from them and bound him before their eyes, keeping him until they should return with Benjamin." },
    { q:"What accusation did Joseph bring, and what proof did he demand?", choices:["Theft, with silver paid in restitution","Treason, with their flocks surrendered","Lying about their father, with proof of his age","That they were spies, proved only by bringing their youngest brother"], a:3, why:"Joseph said, Ye are spies... Hereby ye shall be proved: they must bring their youngest brother so their words could be verified." },
    { q:"What does the brothers' confession — We are verily guilty concerning our brother — reveal about remembered sin?", choices:["Time fully erases the memory of wrongdoing","Misfortune is random and meaningless","Unrepented wrong resurfaces in conscience until it is faced","Only the ringleader bears guilt"], a:2, why:"In distress they said they were verily guilty concerning Joseph, whose anguish they had seen and whose pleas they would not hear." }
  ] },
  { n:43, summary:"When the corn is gone, Judah prevails on Jacob: the man in Egypt warned that they would not see his face without Benjamin. Judah pledges himself as surety — of my hand shalt thou require him — and Israel yields, sending a present of balm, honey, spices, myrrh, nuts, and almonds, double money, and Benjamin, praying, God Almighty give you mercy before the man. Joseph sees Benjamin, orders a feast at his house, and the brothers fearfully explain about the returned money; the steward reassures them and brings Simeon out. Joseph asks after their father, is moved at the sight of his mother's son, and weeps in his chamber before washing his face. They sit by birth order, marvelling, and Benjamin's mess is five times as large as any other.",
  context:"A second journey to Egypt happens when the famine is sore in Canaan and the corn from the first trip is gone.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 43:9", text:"I will be surety for him; of my hand shalt thou require him: if I bring him not unto thee, and set him before thee, then let me bear the blame for ever:" },
    { ref:"Genesis 43:14", text:"And God Almighty give you mercy before the man, that he may send away your other brother, and Benjamin. If I be bereaved of my children, I am bereaved." },
    { ref:"Genesis 43:30", text:"And Joseph made haste; for his bowels did yearn upon his brother: and he sought where to weep; and he entered into his chamber, and wept there." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Covenant love takes personal responsibility for those placed in our trust.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which brother pledged himself as surety for Benjamin?", choices:["Reuben","Judah","Gad","Dan"], a:1, why:"Judah said, I will be surety for him; of my hand shalt thou require him — the same Judah who once proposed selling Joseph." },
    { q:"What present did Israel send down to the man in Egypt?", choices:["Balm, honey, spices, myrrh, nuts, and almonds, with double money","Cattle from the finest herds","A new coat of many colours","Grain from Canaan's stores"], a:0, why:"Israel told them to take of the best fruits in the land — balm, honey, spices, myrrh, nuts, and almonds — and double money in their hand." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's sending of Benjamin with the prayer, God Almighty give you mercy before the man, teach about entrusting outcomes to God?", choices:["Faithful people never face hard partings","Mercy is guaranteed by good planning","Parents should never release their children","After doing all we can, we leave the outcome with God's mercy"], a:3, why:"Israel took counsel, sent the present and his sons, and prayed for mercy before the man, adding, If I be bereaved of my children, I am bereaved." }
  ] },
  { n:44, summary:"Joseph tests his brothers once more: the steward fills their sacks, returns their money, and hides Joseph's silver cup in Benjamin's sack. Chased down soon after leaving the city, the brothers protest innocence, vowing death for the guilty one — and the cup is found in the youngest's sack. They return and fall before Joseph, who insists only Benjamin stay as his servant while the rest go in peace to their father. Then comes Judah's plea, recalling their father's love for the child of his old age, whose life is bound up in the lad's life, and how Jacob will die if Benjamin is lost. Judah, who guaranteed the boy's safety, offers to abide as a bondman instead of the lad, and let the lad go up with his brethren.",
  context:"The testing continues in Joseph's house in Egypt, on the road out of the city, and back before Joseph, the governor.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 44:2", text:"And put my cup, the silver cup, in the sack's mouth of the youngest, and his corn money. And he did according to the word that Joseph had spoken." },
    { ref:"Genesis 44:16", text:"And Judah said, What shall we say unto my lord? what shall we speak? or how shall we clear ourselves? God hath found out the iniquity of thy servants: behold, we are my lord's servants, both we, and he also with whom the cup is found." },
    { ref:"Genesis 44:33", text:"Now therefore, I pray thee, let thy servant abide instead of the lad a bondman to my lord; and let the lad go up with his brethren." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Real repentance shows in sacrifice — standing in for another at real cost to ourselves.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where was Joseph's silver cup found?", choices:["In the steward's own wagon","Buried in the present for Jacob","In Benjamin's sack's mouth","Under Judah's cloak"], a:2, why:"The steward searched, beginning at the eldest and leaving at the youngest, and the cup was found in Benjamin's sack." },
    { q:"Why did Judah offer to remain as Joseph's bondman?", choices:["He hoped to gain standing in Egypt","Lest his father, whose life is bound up in the lad's life, die of sorrow; Judah had become surety for him","He felt guilty about the returned money","Egyptian law demanded a substitute"], a:1, why:"Judah pleaded that his father's life is bound up in the lad's life, and offered himself instead so the lad might go up with his brethren." },
    { q:"How had Judah changed from the brother who proposed selling Joseph?", choices:["He now gives up his own freedom to spare his father and Benjamin","He avoids all involvement in family crises","He relies on wealth to settle disputes","He has learned to hide hard truths"], a:0, why:"Once he said, let us sell him; now he says, let thy servant abide instead of the lad — willing substitution instead of betrayal." }
  ] },
  { n:45, summary:"Unable to refrain himself, Joseph clears the room and makes himself known, weeping aloud until the Egyptians and Pharaoh's house hear. His stunned brothers cannot answer, but Joseph turns their guilt into doctrine: be not grieved, nor angry with yourselves, for God did send me before you to preserve life. Five years of famine remain, and God sent him to preserve a posterity and save lives by a great deliverance, making him a father to Pharaoh and ruler throughout Egypt. He urges them to bring Jacob to dwell in Goshen near him. Pharaoh endorses the move, promising the good of the land and providing wagons. Joseph sends gifts, Benjamin receiving three hundred pieces of silver and five changes of raiment and Jacob ten asses laden with the good things of Egypt, with the counsel, See that ye fall not out by the way. When Jacob sees the wagons, his spirit revives: It is enough; Joseph my son is yet alive: I will go and see him before I die.",
  context:"The recognition scene unfolds in Joseph's house in Egypt; Pharaoh then offers the good of the land for Jacob's family.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 45:5", text:"Now therefore be not grieved, nor angry with yourselves, that ye sold me hither: for God did send me before you to preserve life." },
    { ref:"Genesis 45:7", text:"And God sent me before you to preserve you a posterity in the earth, and to save your lives by a great deliverance." },
    { ref:"Genesis 45:26", text:"And told him, saying, Joseph is yet alive, and he is governor over all the land of Egypt. And Jacob's heart fainted, for he believed them not." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Forgiveness sees God's hand beyond others' offenses: what they meant for harm, God used to preserve life.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many years of famine did Joseph say still remained?", choices:["Two","Three","Seven","Five"], a:3, why:"Joseph said, For these two years hath the famine been in the land: and yet there are five years, in the which there shall neither be earing nor harvest." },
    { q:"How did Joseph explain his brothers' selling him into Egypt?", choices:["It was not you that sent me hither, but God, who sent me before you to preserve life","It happened because he had told his dreams too proudly","Potiphar's household had arranged it","Egypt needed more Hebrew servants"], a:0, why:"Joseph said, God did send me before you to preserve life... so now it was not you that sent me hither, but God." },
    { q:"What does the reconciliation — kissing, weeping, and talking with his brethren — teach about forgiveness?", choices:["Serious betrayal can never be forgiven","Forgiveness waits for full payment","Genuine forgiveness restores relationship instead of exacting payment","Family bonds survive without any change of heart"], a:2, why:"Joseph fell on Benjamin's neck and wept, kissed all his brethren, and after that his brethren talked with him." }
  ] },
  { n:46, summary:"At Beer-sheba Israel offers sacrifices, and God speaks in visions of the night: fear not to go down into Egypt, for there I will make of thee a great nation; I will go down with thee, and I will also surely bring thee up again. Jacob's whole household travels in Pharaoh's wagons, and the chapter lists by name all who entered Egypt: the sons and grandsons of Leah, thirty and three souls with Dinah; of Zilpah, sixteen; of Rachel, fourteen, including Joseph's Egyptian-born sons Manasseh and Ephraim; and of Bilhah, seven — threescore and six in all, threescore and ten with Joseph and his two sons. Judah is sent ahead to direct the way to Goshen, where Joseph meets his father, falls on his neck, and weeps. Israel is content to die, and Joseph coaches the family to tell Pharaoh their trade is cattle, since every shepherd is an abomination unto the Egyptians.",
  context:"Israel journeys from Beer-sheba to Egypt, settling by divine direction in the land of Goshen with all his seed.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 46:3", text:"And he said, I am God, the God of thy father: fear not to go down into Egypt; for I will there make of thee a great nation:" },
    { ref:"Genesis 46:4", text:"I will go down with thee into Egypt; and I will also surely bring thee up again: and Joseph shall put his hand upon thine eyes." },
    { ref:"Genesis 46:27", text:"And the sons of Joseph, which were born him in Egypt, were two souls: all the souls of the house of Jacob, which came into Egypt, were threescore and ten." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God goes with His covenant people into the unknown, and He keeps His word to bring them through.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How many souls does the chapter count of the house of Jacob that came into Egypt?", choices:["Forty and two","Threescore and ten","An hundred","Threescore and six"], a:1, why:"All the souls that came with Jacob out of his loins were threescore and six; with Joseph and his two sons born in Egypt, the house of Jacob was threescore and ten." },
    { q:"Which son did Israel send ahead to direct the way to Goshen?", choices:["Reuben","Levi","Judah","Benjamin"], a:2, why:"He sent Judah before him unto Joseph, to direct his face unto Goshen; and they came into the land of Goshen." },
    { q:"Why were the family to tell Pharaoh their occupation was cattle?", choices:["That they might dwell in the land of Goshen, for every shepherd is an abomination unto the Egyptians","Because Pharaoh was recruiting shepherds","Because they meant to sell all their flocks","Because their herds had died in Canaan"], a:0, why:"Joseph coached them to say their trade had been about cattle from their youth, that they might dwell in Goshen, shepherds being an abomination to Egyptians." }
  ] },
  { n:47, summary:"Joseph presents five of his brothers to Pharaoh, who asks their occupation; they answer that they are shepherds, and Pharaoh grants them the best of the land, Goshen, offering able men as rulers over his cattle. Jacob blesses Pharaoh twice and describes his hundred and thirty years as few and evil compared with his fathers. Joseph settles the family in Rameses and nourishes them with bread. As the famine rages, Joseph gathers all the money of Egypt and Canaan for Pharaoh, then trades bread for cattle, and finally for land and bodies, so the land becomes Pharaoh's and the people move to cities, the priests alone keeping their portion. Joseph gives seed and sets a law of a fifth to Pharaoh, and the people say, Thou hast saved our lives. Israel multiplies in Goshen; Jacob lives seventeen years there, and at a hundred and forty-seven he makes Joseph swear to bury him with his fathers, not in Egypt.",
  context:"Jacob's family settles in Goshen, the best of the land, while Joseph manages Egypt's economy through the famine.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 47:9", text:"And Jacob said unto Pharaoh, The days of the years of my pilgrimage are an hundred and thirty years: few and evil have the days of the years of my life been, and have not attained unto the days of the years of the life of my fathers in the days of their pilgrimage." },
    { ref:"Genesis 47:27", text:"And Israel dwelt in the land of Egypt, in the country of Goshen; and they had possessions therein, and grew, and multiplied exceedingly." },
    { ref:"Genesis 47:30", text:"But I will lie with my fathers, and thou shalt carry me out of Egypt, and bury me in their buryingplace. And he said, I will do as thou hast said." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Faithful stewardship in crisis can preserve both a nation and a family.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did Jacob do when he was set before Pharaoh?", choices:["Blessed him","Presented five of his brothers as interpreters","Asked for a house in Rameses","Recited the family genealogy"], a:0, why:"Joseph brought in Jacob his father, and set him before Pharaoh: and Jacob blessed Pharaoh — and blessed him again as he went out." },
    { q:"What sequence of exchanges did Joseph make as the famine wore on?", choices:["Silver for corn, then cattle for freedom, then land for the priests","Corn for silver, then bread for cattle, then land and servitude for Pharaoh","Land for cattle, then cattle for silver, then silver for the priests","Cattle for corn, then corn for seed, with no further exchange"], a:2, why:"Joseph gathered the money, then traded bread for cattle, and when that year ended took land and the people for Pharaoh, giving them seed and a fifth as rent." },
    { q:"What does Jacob's charge — bury me not, I pray thee, in Egypt — reveal about his hope?", choices:["Egyptian burials were unlawful","Tombs matter more than family","He preferred Canaan's climate","Covenant people look toward the promised land even in death"], a:3, why:"Israel made Joseph swear to carry him out of Egypt to his fathers' buryingplace, and Israel bowed himself upon the bed's head." }
  ] },
  { n:48, summary:"When Jacob falls sick, Joseph brings his two sons to the dying patriarch. Jacob rehearses God's appearance at Luz, then adopts Ephraim and Manasseh as his own — as Reuben and Simeon they shall be mine — and remembers Rachel's death on the way to Ephrath. Israel's eyes are dim with age; he kisses the boys, marvelling that God has shown him his seed. Guiding his hands wittingly, he crosses them: the right hand on younger Ephraim's head, the left on Manasseh the firstborn. Joseph tries to correct the placement, but his father refuses: Manasseh shall be great, but Ephraim greater, his seed a multitude of nations. Israel blesses the lads to grow into a multitude in the midst of the earth, promises God will bring Joseph's family again to the land of their fathers, and gives Joseph one portion above his brethren.",
  context:"The scene takes place in Egypt as the dying Israel blesses Joseph's sons, who were born there before the famine came.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 48:5", text:"And now thy two sons, Ephraim and Manasseh, which were born unto thee in the land of Egypt before I came unto thee into Egypt, are mine; as Reuben and Simeon, they shall be mine." },
    { ref:"Genesis 48:16", text:"The Angel which redeemed me from all evil, bless the lads; and let my name be named on them, and the name of my fathers Abraham and Isaac; and let them grow into a multitude in the midst of the earth." },
    { ref:"Genesis 48:19", text:"And his father refused, and said, I know it, my son, I know it: he also shall become a people, and he also shall be great: but truly his younger brother shall be greater than he, and his seed shall become a multitude of nations." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God's purposes sometimes overturn human expectations of order, choosing the younger for greater work.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"On whose head did Israel set his right hand?", choices:["Manasseh the firstborn","Joseph","Ephraim the younger","Benjamin"], a:2, why:"Israel stretched out his right hand and laid it upon Ephraim's head, who was the younger, guiding his hands wittingly." },
    { q:"What did Jacob mean by calling Ephraim and Manasseh mine; as Reuben and Simeon?", choices:["He was disinheriting his eldest sons","He was assigning them servants in Egypt","He was granting them a portion of Goshen","He was adopting them as his own sons, with standing like his firstborn"], a:3, why:"Jacob said thy two sons... are mine; as Reuben and Simeon, they shall be mine, raising Joseph's sons to full standing in Israel." },
    { q:"What does the crossed blessing of Ephraim and Manasseh — guiding his hands wittingly — show about the Lord's direction?", choices:["Blessings must always follow birth order","Inspiration, not custom, may direct the Lord's blessings, as Jacob himself once knew","Old age prevents prophetic insight","Fathers should refuse correction"], a:1, why:"Though Joseph tried to switch his hands, Jacob refused: Manasseh should be great, but Ephraim greater — as the younger Jacob had been chosen before Esau." }
  ] },
  { n:49, summary:"Gathering his twelve sons, Jacob tells each what shall befall in the last days. Reuben, firstborn, is unstable as water and will not excel because of defiling his father's bed; Simeon and Levi are scattered in Israel for instruments of cruelty and fierce anger. Judah receives the lion's blessing: his brethren shall praise him, and the sceptre shall not depart from Judah, nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be. Zebulun, Issachar, Dan, Gad, Asher, and Naphtali receive shorter prophetic sketches. Joseph is a fruitful bough by a well whose branches run over the wall, blessed above his progenitors' blessings; Benjamin ravens as a wolf. Jacob charges all to bury him in the cave of Machpelah with Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Rebekah, and Leah, then yields up the ghost.",
  context:"The dying Israel blesses his twelve sons in Egypt, foretelling the twelve tribes, and asks burial at Machpelah in Canaan.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 49:10", text:"The sceptre shall not depart from Judah, nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be." },
    { ref:"Genesis 49:28", text:"All these are the twelve tribes of Israel: and this is it that their father spake unto them, and blessed them; every one according to his blessing he blessed them." },
    { ref:"Genesis 49:33", text:"And when Jacob had made an end of commanding his sons, he gathered up his feet into the bed, and yielded up the ghost, and was gathered unto his people." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Blessings follow character: our choices shape what we and our descendants may become.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which son received the promise that the sceptre would not depart from his line?", choices:["Reuben","Joseph","Dan","Judah"], a:3, why:"Judah's blessing ran, The sceptre shall not depart from Judah, nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come." },
    { q:"Why were Simeon and Levi to be divided and scattered in Israel?", choices:["They married Canaanite women","Because in their anger they slew a man, and in their selfwill they digged down a wall","They refused to return to Canaan","They sold Joseph into Egypt"], a:1, why:"Jacob cursed their fierce anger and cruelty — instruments of cruelty in their habitations — recalling the slaughter at Shechem." },
    { q:"What does the promise, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be, point toward?", choices:["A return of the family to Haran","The scattering of Israel among nations","The coming ruler in whom the gathering of God's people would center","Judah's kings conquering Egypt"], a:2, why:"The sceptre would stay with Judah's line until Shiloh come, and unto him the gathering of the people be — a promise of a coming ruler." }
  ] },
  { n:50, summary:"Joseph weeps over his father and has the physicians embalm Israel; forty days are fulfilled, and the Egyptians mourn seventy days. With Pharaoh's leave, a great company of elders and chariots carries Jacob beyond Jordan to the threshingfloor of Atad for seven days of mourning so great the Canaanites name it Abel-mizraim, then buries him in the cave of Machpelah as charged. Back in Egypt, the brothers fear Joseph will now requite their old evil; they send word of their father's command and fall before him as servants. Joseph weeps and answers, Fear not: for am I in the place of God? Ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to save much people alive. He nourishes them and their little ones. Joseph lives a hundred and ten years, sees Ephraim's children of the third generation, and makes Israel swear to carry up his bones when God shall surely visit them; then he dies and is put in a coffin in Egypt.",
  context:"The account closes in Egypt and in Canaan, where Jacob is buried at Machpelah; Joseph dies in Egypt at a hundred and ten.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Genesis 50:20", text:"But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive." },
    { ref:"Genesis 50:24", text:"And Joseph said unto his brethren, I die: and God will surely visit you, and bring you out of this land unto the land which he sware to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob." },
    { ref:"Genesis 50:25", text:"And Joseph took an oath of the children of Israel, saying, God will surely visit you, and ye shall carry up my bones from hence." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God can turn intended evil into lasting good; leaving judgment to Him frees us to nourish and forgive.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How long did the Egyptians mourn for Jacob?", choices:["Seventy days","Forty days","Seven days","Thirty days"], a:0, why:"Forty days were fulfilled for the embalming, and the Egyptians mourned for him threescore and ten days." },
    { q:"How did Joseph answer his brothers' plea for forgiveness after Jacob's death?", choices:["He required seven more years of service","He banished them from Goshen","He delayed judgment until God should visit them","Am I in the place of God? Ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good"], a:3, why:"Joseph wept, said, Fear not: for am I in the place of God?, and spoke kindly, promising to nourish them and their little ones." },
    { q:"Why did Joseph make the children of Israel swear to carry up his bones from Egypt?", choices:["Egyptian embalming had failed","His hope was bound to the land God sware to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, when God should surely visit them","He feared grave robbers","Egyptian law forbade his burial there"], a:1, why:"Joseph died in faith that God will surely visit you, and ye shall carry up my bones from hence — later done when Israel left Egypt (Exodus 13:19)." }
  ] } );
